1
00:00:09,919 --> 00:00:15,639
All right, let's get into error
handling at the link layer.

2
00:00:15,639 --> 00:00:20,944
So, as a reminder, the problem we're
trying to solve here is the fact that

3
00:00:20,944 --> 00:00:26,520
we're transmitting signals over some
physical medium, wired, wireless.

4
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:30,573
And the assumption is they're not perfect,

5
00:00:30,573 --> 00:00:34,632
that there can be some
of errors that happen.

6
00:00:34,632 --> 00:00:40,487
And in the digital world, what this means
is that some bits might get flipped,

7
00:00:40,487 --> 00:00:43,956
might get flipped from a 0 to a 1 or
a 1 to a 0.

8
00:00:43,956 --> 00:00:46,966
And so what we need, at the very least,

9
00:00:46,966 --> 00:00:50,811
is to be able to detect
that an error has occurred.

10
00:00:50,811 --> 00:00:55,636
Better yet, if we can correct it,
that's great.

11
00:00:55,636 --> 00:01:02,530
So one common approach to being able
to detect errors is through parity.

12
00:01:02,530 --> 00:01:07,056
So shown here are just
examples of two values.

13
00:01:07,056 --> 00:01:11,269
Well, it's the same value
being transmitted, but

14
00:01:11,269 --> 00:01:16,366
in one case, one of the bits got
flipped and it caused an error.

15
00:01:16,366 --> 00:01:21,041
And in the other case, it was correct.

16
00:01:21,041 --> 00:01:23,787
All right, so let me pull up a pen.

17
00:01:23,787 --> 00:01:29,681
So our goal is to detect if there
was an error in the transmission.

18
00:01:29,681 --> 00:01:35,714
But how do we know if we just
received these bits right here?

19
00:01:35,714 --> 00:01:40,616
I don't know if the 1 was
actually intended or not.

20
00:01:40,616 --> 00:01:45,237
So what parity does is it
forces every transmission

21
00:01:45,237 --> 00:01:49,751
to have an even number of
1s by adding an extra 1 or

22
00:01:49,751 --> 00:01:56,105
an extra bit set to either 0 or
1 based on the number of 1s in the data.

23
00:01:56,105 --> 00:01:59,478
So this would be called even parity.

24
00:01:59,478 --> 00:02:04,123
We can also, as indicated in
the parentheses, have odd parity,

25
00:02:04,123 --> 00:02:08,943
where the goal is to make the transmission
have an odd number of 1s.

26
00:02:08,943 --> 00:02:12,144
And so if a single bit gets flipped,

27
00:02:12,144 --> 00:02:18,015
we would be able to detect whether
the transmission had an error or

28
00:02:18,015 --> 00:02:21,460
not by looking how many 1s are there.

29
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:26,806
And if it's odd, in the case of even
parity, we would say, all right,

30
00:02:26,806 --> 00:02:31,286
that's not what we were expecting,
so there was an error.

31
00:02:31,286 --> 00:02:37,379
So, as an aside, let's look at the
function of or the functionality of xor.

32
00:02:37,379 --> 00:02:39,044
This is a truth table shown right here.

33
00:02:39,044 --> 00:02:44,785
And so XOR is a logic operation.

34
00:02:44,785 --> 00:02:49,047
And so how to read this if
you've never seen a truth

35
00:02:49,047 --> 00:02:52,307
table before is on the top right here.

36
00:02:52,307 --> 00:02:57,102
We have one of the inputs, and
it has two different possible values.

37
00:02:57,102 --> 00:03:00,801
And then on the left,
we have the second input, and

38
00:03:00,801 --> 00:03:04,001
it also has two different possible values.

39
00:03:04,001 --> 00:03:10,416
So in this case, you would say
if the first input is a 0 and

40
00:03:10,416 --> 00:03:16,847
the second input is a 0, so
0 xor 0, the result is a 0.

41
00:03:16,847 --> 00:03:22,498
Likewise, if the first input is a 1 and

42
00:03:22,498 --> 00:03:27,646
the second is a 0, 1 xor 0 is a 1.

43
00:03:27,646 --> 00:03:30,607
And so this tells the whole truth table.

44
00:03:30,607 --> 00:03:35,156
And if you're trying to
do it kind of bit wise,

45
00:03:35,156 --> 00:03:39,356
you would just line the bits up and
with 1,

46
00:03:39,356 --> 00:03:44,268
we know that's a 0, 0,
XOR, 1 with 1 is a 1.

47
00:03:44,268 --> 00:03:46,611
1 xor with 0 is a 1.

48
00:03:46,611 --> 00:03:49,359
0 xor with 0 is a 0.

49
00:03:49,359 --> 00:03:51,202
All right, so why am I telling you this?

50
00:03:51,202 --> 00:03:56,370
Well, it's the function or
the logic that we can

51
00:03:56,370 --> 00:04:02,311
use to easily count the number
of 1s in an array of bits.

52
00:04:02,311 --> 00:04:06,870
So shown here is an example of four bits,
1010.

53
00:04:06,870 --> 00:04:11,569
Visually, we can look at it and
see that there's two 1s, and so

54
00:04:11,569 --> 00:04:13,587
we know that that's even.

55
00:04:13,587 --> 00:04:17,649
But if we were to either use code or
logic,

56
00:04:17,649 --> 00:04:22,421
we can use this XOR
functionality to check this.

57
00:04:22,421 --> 00:04:28,142
So we would do 1 XOR with 0, which is 1,

58
00:04:28,142 --> 00:04:33,044
and then 1 xor with 1 is now 0, and

59
00:04:33,044 --> 00:04:37,631
then now 0 XOR with 0 equals 0.

60
00:04:37,631 --> 00:04:42,306
All right, so that indicates to
us that there was an even number

61
00:04:42,306 --> 00:04:44,964
of 1s in this bit string ring here.

62
00:04:44,964 --> 00:04:49,351
Now, if we look at another example that
we can see has three, should be odd.

63
00:04:49,351 --> 00:04:51,991
Let's double check that using XOR.

64
00:04:51,991 --> 00:04:58,840
So one XOR with 1, we look at the table,
we can see that that's a 0.

65
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:02,378
Then we say 0 XOR with 0.

66
00:05:02,378 --> 00:05:08,210
Now that's 1, and
then one XOR with 0 is now 1.

67
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:10,156
And that's odd, all right?

68
00:05:10,156 --> 00:05:15,124
So now we know how to be able to
calculate whether there's an even or

69
00:05:15,124 --> 00:05:18,248
an odd number of bits in some bit pattern.

70
00:05:18,248 --> 00:05:21,379
So let's go through a concrete example.

71
00:05:21,379 --> 00:05:25,575
And here we're going to use
specifically even parity.

72
00:05:25,575 --> 00:05:30,264
That means that our
transmission has to have

73
00:05:30,264 --> 00:05:35,089
an equal number or
an even number of 1s in it.

74
00:05:35,089 --> 00:05:38,029
All right, so let's look at this.

75
00:05:38,029 --> 00:05:44,152
input data, that's the data that
we want to be able to transmit.

76
00:05:44,152 --> 00:05:49,440
We would go through and
determine by xorring each of these

77
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:55,292
bits to see how many is it currently
even or is it currently odd?

78
00:05:55,292 --> 00:06:00,558
And we can see that the data that
we want to transmit ends up with

79
00:06:00,558 --> 00:06:05,434
0 when we XOR all of them,
meaning it's already even.

80
00:06:05,434 --> 00:06:09,698
So what we're going to do is we're
going to add a 0 to the transmission.

81
00:06:09,698 --> 00:06:16,316
So now with that one extra bit,
which is shown here at the end,

82
00:06:16,316 --> 00:06:23,081
we can see that the transmitted
number of bits is going to be even.

83
00:06:23,081 --> 00:06:29,764
All right, so as another example,
here's the data we want to transmit.

84
00:06:29,764 --> 00:06:36,631
So to calculate whether this has an even
or odd number of 1s, we would XOR them.

85
00:06:36,631 --> 00:06:41,311
So we would 0, XOR, 1,
XOR with 0 and so forth.

86
00:06:41,311 --> 00:06:46,342
And we can see that the end result is 1,
meaning this value

87
00:06:46,342 --> 00:06:51,801
right here that we want to transmit
has an odd number of 1s in it.

88
00:06:51,801 --> 00:06:54,237
So it's odd, so to make it even,

89
00:06:54,237 --> 00:06:59,197
what we're going to do is we're going to
add a one to this transmission.

90
00:06:59,197 --> 00:07:06,808
So we take the original value and
then we append on the value 1 onto it,

91
00:07:06,808 --> 00:07:13,388
making it this whole value,
have an even number of 1s in it.

92
00:07:13,388 --> 00:07:16,435
So how do we use that
on the receiving side?

93
00:07:16,435 --> 00:07:20,563
So let's say now we've
received this value, and

94
00:07:20,563 --> 00:07:25,685
we want to be able to say,
is this what was actually sent or not?

95
00:07:25,685 --> 00:07:27,462
So we would do the same operation.

96
00:07:27,462 --> 00:07:31,115
We would double-check,
we would go through and

97
00:07:31,115 --> 00:07:34,421
XOR every single bit, including this bit.

98
00:07:34,421 --> 00:07:38,578
And if the end result is 0,
we know it was correct,

99
00:07:38,578 --> 00:07:43,709
because that indicates this
transmission had an even number,

100
00:07:43,709 --> 00:07:48,256
which both sides agreed to,
that we're going to make sure

101
00:07:48,256 --> 00:07:53,131
that that's the case, and so
this ended up being correct.

102
00:07:53,131 --> 00:07:57,205
Looking at the second example
we had on the previous slide,

103
00:07:57,205 --> 00:08:00,148
we can go through the exact same exercise.

104
00:08:00,148 --> 00:08:01,241
We can XOR.

105
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:07,928
All of these bits, including this extra
parity bit and we would see the end

106
00:08:07,928 --> 00:08:13,358
result is zero, meaning that there's
an even number of ones in here.

107
00:08:13,358 --> 00:08:15,812
So we can do this kind of manually.

108
00:08:15,812 --> 00:08:20,446
One, two, three, four, five,
six, six is an even number.

109
00:08:20,446 --> 00:08:23,054
So our math is working.

110
00:08:23,054 --> 00:08:27,302
So that means this value was correctly
transmitted and received it.

111
00:08:27,302 --> 00:08:29,220
There's no errors.

112
00:08:29,220 --> 00:08:31,366
Now let's look at this value.

113
00:08:31,366 --> 00:08:34,095
But let's say one of the bits flipped,

114
00:08:34,095 --> 00:08:37,660
but we don't know that
this is actually flipped.

115
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:40,790
We wouldn't receive a red zero or
anything like that.

116
00:08:40,790 --> 00:08:44,870
So we would go through and
we would XOR each of these bits,

117
00:08:44,870 --> 00:08:48,010
including that bit that flipped was zero.

118
00:08:48,010 --> 00:08:51,642
So what would be the end result is a one.

119
00:08:51,642 --> 00:08:57,020
There's now an odd number of ones,
one, two, three, four, five.

120
00:08:57,020 --> 00:09:02,644
And so what that means is I was expecting
it to be even, but it's actually odd.

121
00:09:02,644 --> 00:09:04,860
So I know it's an error.

122
00:09:06,080 --> 00:09:10,875
All right, so parity is great,

123
00:09:10,875 --> 00:09:14,480
but it's limited.

124
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:18,628
So for example,
if there's two errors that occur,

125
00:09:18,628 --> 00:09:24,380
you wouldn't be able to detect that
because it's flipping two values,

126
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:29,018
meaning it goes the first flip
changes it from even to odd.

127
00:09:29,018 --> 00:09:34,500
And then the second flip would
then change it back to even.

128
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:38,285
All right, so a CRC or a cyclic redundancy

129
00:09:38,285 --> 00:09:43,180
check is a more powerful
form of error detection.

130
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:48,932
So cyclic codes are particularly
suited to detect burst errors.

131
00:09:48,932 --> 00:09:53,960
And it's been adopted for
the FCS field in Ethernet.

132
00:09:55,020 --> 00:09:58,780
So let's go through how to calculate this,
and

133
00:09:58,780 --> 00:10:03,826
you might need to kind of look at
this extra material on the web.

134
00:10:03,826 --> 00:10:09,382
I provide a link at the end of a really
good website that overviews this.

135
00:10:09,382 --> 00:10:13,158
But we'll go through an example here.

136
00:10:13,158 --> 00:10:17,260
All right, so
just kind of this shows the setup.

137
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:22,063
All right, so
D is the data to be transmitted and

138
00:10:22,063 --> 00:10:27,520
then G is going to be called
a generator polynomial.

139
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:31,900
And this is some value defined
by the Ethernet standard.

140
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:37,133
Then our job as part of
calculating the CRC is to find

141
00:10:37,133 --> 00:10:44,286
these extra bits that we're going
to add to it, which we'll call R.

142
00:10:44,286 --> 00:10:50,696
So that we're going to calculate R,
where DR is what will be transmitted.

143
00:10:50,696 --> 00:10:57,428
And our goal is to make it
find the value such that DR,

144
00:10:57,428 --> 00:11:04,180
which viewed as all as one,
is divisible by G.

145
00:11:04,180 --> 00:11:11,642
All right, so the check would be then
we've received this whole value.

146
00:11:11,642 --> 00:11:15,455
We also divide it by G, and
if the remainder is zero,

147
00:11:15,455 --> 00:11:19,460
that there is no error,
otherwise there was an error.

148
00:11:20,520 --> 00:11:22,782
All right, so let's go through an example.

149
00:11:22,782 --> 00:11:26,164
We're going to use R equals three for
three bits.

150
00:11:26,164 --> 00:11:30,380
So not capital r but r bits.

151
00:11:30,380 --> 00:11:34,826
We're going to use the polynomial 1010 and

152
00:11:34,826 --> 00:11:39,950
we're going to use
the data value 11101011.

153
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:45,710
And our goal is to find R.

154
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:49,964
Actually, this is incorrect.

155
00:11:49,964 --> 00:11:51,170
This is a zero.

156
00:11:53,140 --> 00:11:55,334
So ignore this last bit.

157
00:11:55,334 --> 00:11:58,102
But let's go through this example.

158
00:11:58,102 --> 00:12:03,800
So how we do this is here's
the data to be transmitted and

159
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:09,080
then we're going to start with R as zeros.

160
00:12:09,080 --> 00:12:13,806
And so this is going to allow
us to determine what we need to

161
00:12:13,806 --> 00:12:17,850
transmit to make DR to be
exactly divisible by G.

162
00:12:18,940 --> 00:12:25,280
So once we've done that,
we can now start going doing division.

163
00:12:25,280 --> 00:12:29,090
And this looks like it
would be hard to implement,

164
00:12:29,090 --> 00:12:33,680
but actually it's just
involving an XOR operation.

165
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:40,534
So it actually turns out to be a pretty
straightforward algorithm to calculate,

166
00:12:40,534 --> 00:12:46,188
but to illustrate it,
we show it as kind of doing long division.

167
00:12:46,188 --> 00:12:49,478
All right, so here's our data.

168
00:12:49,478 --> 00:12:51,220
Here's these R bits.

169
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:54,870
Now here's G.

170
00:12:56,120 --> 00:13:00,147
All right, so DRG, and
we're trying to find R,

171
00:13:00,147 --> 00:13:06,148
said initially to be all zeros and
we're going to find the remainder.

172
00:13:06,148 --> 00:13:11,407
All right, so how we can do this division

173
00:13:11,407 --> 00:13:15,892
is if the value G can fit into this,

174
00:13:15,892 --> 00:13:21,461
whatever the bits that we're looking at,

175
00:13:21,461 --> 00:13:26,800
then the quotient is going to be a one.

176
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:31,061
And if it can't fit into it,
basically, meaning this needs to be

177
00:13:31,061 --> 00:13:34,867
four bits starting with a one,
because this Is four bits.

178
00:13:34,867 --> 00:13:38,299
And we'll go through and
we'll see examples of that,

179
00:13:38,299 --> 00:13:41,000
otherwise the value will be zero.

180
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:47,226
And so modulo two division simply
involves the XOR operation, all right?

181
00:13:47,226 --> 00:13:52,306
So first bit that would fit is
once we get to here, and so

182
00:13:52,306 --> 00:13:57,392
that means this is our first bit,
that's an zero one.

183
00:13:57,392 --> 00:14:00,982
Doing the kind of division operation,

184
00:14:00,982 --> 00:14:06,408
we put the 1010 here and
then we XOR these two values.

185
00:14:06,408 --> 00:14:10,909
So zero XOR zero is zero,
one XOR one is zero,

186
00:14:10,909 --> 00:14:16,306
one XOR zero is one and
one XOR one is zero, all right?

187
00:14:16,306 --> 00:14:24,500
So then we carry the next or pull the next
bit down, and so now we have 1001.

188
00:14:24,500 --> 00:14:28,690
And do we say this can fit into that?

189
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:31,194
Yes, it can.

190
00:14:31,194 --> 00:14:35,944
So then that bits of one, we multiply

191
00:14:35,944 --> 00:14:40,912
that down 1010 XOR these values.

192
00:14:40,912 --> 00:14:45,841
So one XOR zero is zero,
zero XOR one is one,

193
00:14:45,841 --> 00:14:51,500
zero XOR zero is zero,
and one XOR one is zero.

194
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:59,467
All right, so then this would be a case
where even if we pull down the zero,

195
00:14:59,467 --> 00:15:05,390
this can't fit into, so
G cannot fit into this value.

196
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:10,413
All right, this is only three bits with
that leading one, this is four bits, so

197
00:15:10,413 --> 00:15:11,382
it can't fit.

198
00:15:11,382 --> 00:15:16,022
So at this point,
this quotient bit is a zero, all right?

199
00:15:16,022 --> 00:15:19,700
So then we would pull down the next bit,

200
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:25,226
we would pull the next bit down
to make it a one, all right?

201
00:15:25,226 --> 00:15:30,042
So let's look at where we left off
instead of going through all of this.

202
00:15:30,042 --> 00:15:34,174
So we had this one right
there that we pulled down.

203
00:15:34,174 --> 00:15:37,820
So this is where we left
off is right there.

204
00:15:37,820 --> 00:15:40,542
So we now pull down a one.

205
00:15:40,542 --> 00:15:47,420
Now it fits do the modulo two division.

206
00:15:47,420 --> 00:15:55,340
So one XOR zero,
one zero XOR one one and so forth.

207
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:58,716
Now, if we keep going,
going through all of this,

208
00:15:58,716 --> 00:16:02,675
we're going to end up
with a value down here.

209
00:16:02,675 --> 00:16:05,214
That's the remainder, all right?

210
00:16:05,214 --> 00:16:09,714
And so, this then becomes,

211
00:16:09,714 --> 00:16:14,408
this 110 is the value of R.

212
00:16:14,408 --> 00:16:18,138
And so, that's what we would
add on to the transmission.

213
00:16:18,138 --> 00:16:24,308
So, we'd take our original value of D,
which is actually a zero,

214
00:16:24,308 --> 00:16:29,727
pend it on R, and
then that's what we're transmitting.

215
00:16:29,727 --> 00:16:35,011
And much as with parity, so
that's what we're transmit.

216
00:16:35,011 --> 00:16:42,423
Now, much with parity on the check side,
we basically do the exact same operation.

217
00:16:42,423 --> 00:16:46,813
So this is corrected,
we do the exact same operation.

218
00:16:46,813 --> 00:16:54,063
We take this value,
all of it, divide it by G.

219
00:16:54,063 --> 00:16:56,935
And once we get down to the bottom,

220
00:16:56,935 --> 00:17:01,491
if the remainder that we
would calculate is all zeros,

221
00:17:01,491 --> 00:17:07,157
then we knew it was correct,
because it was exactly divisible by G.

222
00:17:07,157 --> 00:17:13,228
Otherwise, there's an error
in our transmission.

223
00:17:13,228 --> 00:17:19,689
So what are some properties
of CRCs in Ethernet?

224
00:17:19,689 --> 00:17:24,460
So it detects any 1 bit error,

225
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:32,543
it also detects any two
adjacent single bit errors.

226
00:17:32,543 --> 00:17:37,320
It detects any odd number
of single bit errors, and

227
00:17:37,320 --> 00:17:42,111
any burst ff errors with
the length of 32 or less.

228
00:17:42,111 --> 00:17:48,016
So that's pretty powerful,
that's much better than parity.

229
00:17:48,016 --> 00:17:53,452
And it's also important, because it can be
calculated as bytes arrive off the wire.

230
00:17:53,452 --> 00:17:57,019
So this is something that can
have a simple hardware or

